  "rationale": "The requirement `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.outgoing_edges.fn` says that criterion code uses `ValidationContext::outgoing_edges` for outgoing-edge selection. Its only structural obligation is that this operation exists with the signature given in `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.ValidationContext.outgoing_edges`. I assessed that obligation only. The runtime behavior, inputs and MCP-tool requirements are separate declarations that I did not assess here.\n\n**Signature.** The implementation root is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs#impl[ValidationContext<'d, D>]#2/fn[outgoing_edges]`.\n- It sits in `impl<'d, D: Domain> ValidationContext<'d, D>`, as the spec requires.\n- It is `pub fn outgoing_edges(&self, node: &D::NodeId, label: D::EdgeLabel) -> Result<Snapshot<Vec<D::NodeId>>, D::Error> where D::EdgeLabel: PartialEq`.\n- That matches the specified signature exactly: receiver, parameter types, return type and where-clause.\n\n**Delegation.** The body calls `prepare_edges`. That helper calls `self.graph.outgoing_edges(node, label)` on the `Domain` and builds the snapshot with `edge_fingerprint`. The body then records the observation through `observe_edges` and returns the `Snapshot`. This is the interface-level delegation the operation needs. `successors` in `impl[ValidationContext<'d, D>]#1` also goes through this method, so criterion code has a single entry point for edge selection.\n\n**Marking.** The method carries `{impl}` for both `outgoing_edges.fn` and `ValidationContext.outgoing_edges`. It is the only `{impl}` site for `.fn`. Nothing else needs a reference, because the helpers are reached from the marked item.\n\nThere are no acknowledgments and no `{fixme}` references, and I found no structural gap or ambiguity.",
